    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the codebase. \n\n## Problem Summary\nThe issue was that tax calculations were using Python's default `round()` function, which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even), but the tests expected \"round half away from zero\" behavior. This caused tax amounts like 12.5 cents to be rounded down to 12 instead of up to 13.\n\n## Solution Applied\nI modified the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py` to properly implement \"round half away from zero\" logic instead of using Python's built-in rounding.\n\n## Verification\n- Fixed the specific failing test: `test_reduced_rate_on_250_cents_rounds_up`\n- All previously failing tests now pass\n- All 85 tests in the test suite pass without any regressions\n- Tax calculations now correctly round 12.5 cents up to 13 cents as expected\n\nThe fix ensures that tax computations like 5% of $2.50 (which equals 12.5 cents) now correctly round up to 13 cents, resolving the one-cent discrepancy in invoice tax calculations.",
